---
title: Interactions and Selections
description: Build controlled cursors, linked selections, brushes, zooming, scrolling, timelines, and editors around semantic chart state.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts owns datum focus, selection, crosshair presentation, optional\ncursor bindings, and the mechanics of explicitly imported chart controls. The\napplication owns accepted semantic values, shared controller identity, and\nproduct policy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This boundary keeps the default host small and lets applications use\nbattle-tested interaction controllers only when needed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-owner","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the owner"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use native chart focus for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"nearest-point inspection;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"grouped axis tooltips;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"snapped crosshairs;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"synchronized focus cursors;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keyboard point navigation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"point activation."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a first-party controlled chart control for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"categorical series visibility through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interactiveColorLegend"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"semantic point selection through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyedSelection"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"an unsnapped numeric or temporal plot position through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"continuousCursor"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"one ordered scale value through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"handleX"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a scale-bound horizontal range through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"brushX"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a controlled numeric or temporal x window through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zoomX"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"other controls that explicitly accept a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ControlledSignal"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use controlled application state for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"shared cursor-controller identity and programmatic cursor values;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"accepted brush, zoom, and pan domains;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"focus-and-context windows;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"synchronized-view layout and domain policy;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"scrollable resource lanes;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"playback timing and play/pause controls;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"editable intervals;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rich pinned tooltips."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for chart-owned datum inspection."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"cursors-and-crosshairs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cursors and crosshairs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crosshair"},{"type":"text","value":" is presentation; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" is state. A crosshair without\na cursor binding follows the chart's local "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartFocusState"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { crosshair } from '@tanstack/charts/crosshair'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    crosshair({ x: { label: true }, y: false }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  maxFocusDistance: Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This gives pointer and keyboard users one snapped vertical guide. It does not\ncreate a point, change the focus strategy, or require an SVG overlay."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a categorical axis, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x: { band: true }"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y: { band: true }"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces\nthat axis rule with a cursor band sized from the resolved scale bandwidth. A\nband options object controls inset, radius, fill, stroke, and opacity. Use\nseparate crosshair marks when the band should paint below the data and the\nother axis rule should paint above it. Zero-bandwidth axes emit no band."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"synchronize-focus-by-value","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Synchronize focus by value"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Share one controller between definitions when several browser or React Native\ncharts should resolve the same semantic value through their own scales:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createChartCursor, cursorHost } from '@tanstack/charts/cursor'\n\nconst sharedDate = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\nconst cursor = {\n  use: cursorHost,\n  controller: sharedDate,\n  mode: 'focus' as const,\n  match: 'x' as const,\n  pin: true,\n}\n\nconst throughputDefinition = defineChart(throughputSpec, { cursor })\nconst errorsDefinition = defineChart(errorsSpec, { cursor })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus mode publishes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor: 'value'"},{"type":"text","value":". Each chart resolves that date to its\nown local point and focus group; no chart copies another chart's pixels.\nPointer, keyboard, restored, and programmatic sources remain visible in the\nshared state. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"match"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xy"},{"type":"text","value":" and can be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":". Focus state may\nalso carry an optional local "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin"},{"type":"text","value":" point identity to break ties between\nrepeated equal values, including facets. A stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markId"},{"type":"text","value":" survives\ndata reordering; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datumIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" disambiguates duplicate keys. A chart ignores the\norigin when its key and mark do not exist locally and resolves from the\nportable semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":" and preferred "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"track-free-coordinates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Track free coordinates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Free mode updates without selecting a datum. The host stores normalized plot\ncoordinates and derives semantic values through each resolved scale's inverse:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const freeCursor = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\nconst xScale = scaleUtc().domain([start, end])\nconst yScale = scaleLinear().domain([0, maximum])\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    crosshair({\n      x: { label: true },\n      y: { label: true },\n      marker: true,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  cursor: {\n    use: cursorHost,\n    controller: freeCursor,\n    mode: 'free',\n    pin: true,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The resolved final ranges own inversion, including a reversed y range. Add an\naxis "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"valueAt"},{"type":"text","value":" only to replace that default with observed-value snapping,\nrounding, or another semantic policy. A missing or non-invertible scale\nrequires such an override; returning "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps that axis\ncoordinate-only."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set semantic state directly when another control owns the cursor:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"freeCursor.setState({\n  anchor: 'value',\n  value: { x: selectedDate, y: selectedValue },\n  source: 'programmatic',\n  pinned: true,\n})\n\nfreeCursor.setState(null)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pin: true"},{"type":"text","value":", click or tap pins the current cursor and another activation\ndismisses it. Browser focus mode also supports Enter, Space, and Escape. React\nChart-owned focus exposes equivalent activate and escape accessibility\nactions. Free mode has no invented keyboard or accessibility point order; pair\nit with labeled date/number inputs, a range control, or textual status when\narbitrary values are part of the reader's task. The crosshair itself is visual\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aria-hidden"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Browser renderer hosts and React Native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Chart"},{"type":"text","value":" both bind focus and free\ncursors. They share controller state, semantic focus resolution, coordinate\nprojection, and crosshair presentation; only their event plumbing differs.\nReact Native uses responder gestures and accessibility actions instead of DOM\npointer and key events."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Application code imports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartCursor"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursorHost"},{"type":"text","value":", and cursor state\ntypes from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/cursor"},{"type":"text","value":". Every cursor binding supplies\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"use: cursorHost"},{"type":"text","value":", keeping cursor policy out of charts that do not opt in. The\nadapter-facing\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/cursor/host"},{"type":"text","value":" entry contains the platform-neutral projection\nand focus helpers used to implement a host; ordinary chart definitions do not\nneed it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"controlled-signals","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled signals"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A controlled signal is the boundary between application-owned semantic state\nand a chart-owned behavior:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\nconst visible = controlledSignal(visibleSeries, (next, { reason }) => {\n  setVisibleSeries(next)\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is only a typed snapshot and callback. It does not create a chart store,\nsubscription, or second lifecycle. Rebuild the definition with the accepted\nvalue, as with a controlled form input. The behavior owns interaction details;\nthe application owns persistence and policy."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"controlled-keyed-selection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled keyed selection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyedSelection"},{"type":"text","value":" when chart activation and application UI share one stable\ndatum key:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { keyedSelection, whenSelected } from '@tanstack/charts/selection'\n\nconst selection = keyedSelection<Observation, string, number, number>({\n  selected: controlledSignal(selectedId, (next, { reason }) => {\n    setSelectedId(next)\n  }),\n  key: (datum) => datum.id,\n})\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(observations, {\n      id: 'observations',\n      x: 'flipperLength',\n      y: 'bodyMass',\n      key: 'id',\n    }),\n    whenSelected(\n      dot(observations, {\n        id: 'selected-observation',\n        x: 'flipperLength',\n        y: 'bodyMass',\n        key: 'id',\n        r: 7,\n        strokeWidth: 2,\n      }),\n      selection,\n    ),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  selection,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Click, Enter, and Space propose the selected key through the controlled signal.\nA blank-surface click proposes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" when a selection exists. The change\nreason distinguishes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear"},{"type":"text","value":" and records whether activation came\nfrom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard"},{"type":"text","value":". A nullish key makes that point non-selectable."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenSelected"},{"type":"text","value":" is an ordinary authored mark filtered after scale domains\nresolve. Its complete data and channels still contribute to the domains, but\nonly geometry whose point matches the selected key is painted. The filtered\noverlay is decorative: it emits no second focus or activation point. If one\nlogical point owns several scene fragments, every matching fragment remains."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus and selection are independent. Focus can move without changing the\ncontrolled key, and a selected overlay does not replace the normal focus ring.\nKeep semantic tables, status announcements, clear buttons, persistence, and\nproduct-specific key policy in application UI. Those controls can update the\nsame selected value used to rebuild the definition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"continuous-cursor","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Continuous cursor"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"continuousCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" for an arbitrary x/y plot position that must not snap to\na datum:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  continuousCursor,\n  type ContinuousCursorChange,\n  type ContinuousCursorPosition,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\ntype Position = ContinuousCursorPosition<number, number>\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [dot(rows, { x: 'horsepower', y: 'economy' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: horsepowerScale },\n    y: { scale: economyScale },\n  },\n\n  controls: [\n    continuousCursor({\n      position: controlledSignal<\n        Position | null,\n        ContinuousCursorChange<number, number>\n      >(cursorPosition, (next, { reason }) => {\n        if (reason.type === 'commit' || reason.type === 'clear') {\n          setCursorPosition(next)\n        }\n      }),\n      xLabel: { format: (value) => `HP ${value.toFixed(1)}` },\n      yLabel: { format: (value) => `MPG ${value.toFixed(1)}` },\n    }),\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both scales must be invertible numeric or temporal scales. The behavior uses\ntheir final resolved ranges, including a reversed y range, and clamps values to\nthe plot. This is the same default inversion used by a free "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursorHost"},{"type":"text","value":"\nbinding. Rules and the marker are enabled by default. Axis labels are opt-in."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" controlled position leaves pointer previews transient. Click or tap\nproposes a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":"; accepting its non-null position pins the cursor. A second\nactivation or Escape proposes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear"},{"type":"text","value":". Pointer leave and cancellation clear an\nunpinned preview. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ContinuousCursorChange"},{"type":"text","value":" distinguishes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preview"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":",\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear"},{"type":"text","value":", records pointer, touch, or keyboard source, and preserves the\norigin position."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The SVG and Canvas DOM hosts replace the static scene fallback with a host\noverlay, so pointer movement repaints the guide without rebuilding the chart\nscene. Static SVG and React Native paint an accepted non-null position but do\nnot provide pointer input. Pair the cursor with semantic sliders or inputs and\nvisible status text for keyboard and nonvisual operation. Those controls can\nwrite the same application-owned position."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-interaction-loop","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom interaction loop"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use this lower-level loop when no first-party behavior owns the gesture:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render the definition from semantic state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.chart"},{"type":"text","value":" and resolved scales in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRender"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Convert pointer geometry into semantic values."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clamp, snap, or validate those values as product policy."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update application state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let the next definition produce the scene."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not mutate SVG geometry directly and then attempt to reconcile application\nstate afterward."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"controlled-point-inspection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled point inspection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the chart's interaction controller when the application owns pointer\ntiming but still wants the definition's focus strategy, focus marks, and\ntooltip. Long-press inspection is one example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"let interaction: ChartInteractionController<Row, Date, number> | undefined\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', key: 'id' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc() },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear() },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'nearest-x',\n  pointer: false,\n  tooltip,\n})\n\nconst chart = (\n  <Chart\n    definition={definition}\n    ariaLabel=\"Portfolio history\"\n    onRender={(context) => {\n      interaction = context.interaction\n    }}\n  />\n)\n\nfunction inspect(clientX: number, clientY: number) {\n  interaction?.setControlledFocus(interaction.resolvePointer(clientX, clientY))\n}\n\nfunction stopInspecting() {\n  interaction?.setControlledFocus(null)\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolvePointer"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the current renderer presentation, including an active\nmotion or viewport transform, and returns the scene position, primary point,\nand complete focus group. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setControlledFocus"},{"type":"text","value":" paints the same definition-owned\nfocus and tooltip as chart-owned pointer input. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ pinned: true }"},{"type":"text","value":" when the\nconfigured sticky tooltip should accept interaction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a drag that does not require a nearby datum, use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interaction.clientToScene(clientX, clientY)"},{"type":"text","value":". It applies the renderer's full\nclient-to-scene transform without coupling viewport movement to point focus."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer: false"},{"type":"text","value":" disables automatic pointer move, leave, and click handling. It\ndoes not disable keyboard navigation. Controlled focus has separate ownership,\nso unrelated mouse-leave and focus-out events cannot clear it. The stable\ncontroller is available as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.interaction"},{"type":"text","value":" and in every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRender"},{"type":"text","value":" context."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"invert-resolved-scales","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Invert resolved scales"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First-party behaviors use the final resolved scale. A custom gesture can read\nthe same optional inverse from the scene:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const invertX = scene.scales.x.invert\nif (!invertX) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible x scale')\n\nconst date = invertX(pointerX)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The resolved y scale already owns its reversed pixel range:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const invertY = scene.scales.y.invert\nif (!invertY) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible y scale')\n\nconst value = invertY(pointerY)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply UTC month snapping, numeric rounding, minimum ranges, and domain clamps\nafter inversion. Those policies are application semantics, not scale math.\nThis date example uses a D3 time scale; the lightweight linear scale supports\nthe same resolved mapping and inversion flow for numeric gestures.\nFree cursor bindings already consume the resolved inverse. Their "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"valueAt"},{"type":"text","value":"\ncallback is reserved for snapping or another semantic override."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" page is the sole source for\nD3 ownership and official interaction-module links."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"disable-competing-datum-focus","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable competing datum focus"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a gesture has no datum inspection at all, disable chart-owned focus explicitly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { focusDisabled } from '@tanstack/charts/focus/disabled'\n\nconst gestureDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  focus: focusDisabled,\n  keyboard: false,\n})\n\nmountChart(element, {\n  definition: gestureDefinition,\n  ariaLabel: 'Selectable monthly range',\n  onRender: mountBrushOverlay,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This prevents an application-owned gesture from competing with the host's\npoint marker and tooltip. First-party host controls such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"brushX"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zoomX"},{"type":"text","value":" isolate their own events."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer: false"},{"type":"text","value":" plus the interaction controller when the application owns\nthe gesture but the chart should still own datum focus. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusDisabled"},{"type":"text","value":" does\nnot remove keyboard accessibility from application-owned controls.\nA definition "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursor"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"free"},{"type":"text","value":" mode already owns the host pointer path and\ndoes not require "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer: false"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusDisabled"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"brush-selection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Brush selection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A complete brush owns:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"drag start, move, end, and cancellation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reverse dragging normalization;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"semantic snapping;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a visible selected range;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"focusable handles or equivalent range inputs;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"current-range text;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reset behavior;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"selection preservation after data updates."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import the optional first-party behavior and bind it to application state:"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"controlled-brush-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"controlled-brush","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/App.tsx\",\"environment\":\"charts-react\",\"files\":{\"/src/App.tsx\":\"import { useMemo, useState } from 'react'\\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport {\\n  brushX,\\n  type BrushRange,\\n  type BrushXChange,\\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush'\\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\\nimport { Chart } from '@tanstack/charts/react'\\nimport { rows } from './data'\\n\\nconst weeks = rows.map((row) => row.week)\\nconst initialRange: BrushRange<number> = { start: 2, end: 6 }\\n\\nexport default function App() {\\n  const [range, setRange] = useState<BrushRange<number>>(initialRange)\\n  const definition = useMemo(\\n    () =>\\n      defineChart({\\n        marks: [\\n          lineY(rows, {\\n            x: 'week',\\n            y: 'signups',\\n            points: true,\\n            stroke: '#2563eb',\\n            strokeWidth: 2.5,\\n          }),\\n        ],\\n        scales: {\\n          x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([1, 8]) },\\n          y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Signups' } },\\n        },\\n\\n        controls: [\\n          brushX({\\n            range: controlledSignal<BrushRange<number>, BrushXChange<number>>(\\n              range,\\n              (next, { reason }) => {\\n                if (reason.type === 'commit') setRange(next)\\n              },\\n            ),\\n            values: weeks,\\n            format: (week) => `Week ${week}`,\\n            ariaLabel: 'Reporting range',\\n            startAriaLabel: 'Range start',\\n            endAriaLabel: 'Range end',\\n          }),\\n        ],\\n      }),\\n    [range],\\n  )\\n\\n  const isInitial =\\n    range.start === initialRange.start && range.end === initialRange.end\\n\\n  return (\\n    <section>\\n      <Chart\\n        definition={definition}\\n        height={280}\\n        ariaLabel=\\\"Weekly signups with a selectable reporting range\\\"\\n      />\\n      <p role=\\\"status\\\" aria-live=\\\"polite\\\">\\n        Current range: weeks {range.start}–{range.end}\\n      </p>\\n      <button\\n        type=\\\"button\\\"\\n        disabled={isInitial}\\n        onClick={() => setRange({ ...initialRange })}\\n      >\\n        Reset range\\n      </button>\\n    </section>\\n  )\\n}\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export const rows = [\\n  { week: 1, signups: 18 },\\n  { week: 2, signups: 24 },\\n  { week: 3, signups: 31 },\\n  { week: 4, signups: 29 },\\n  { week: 5, signups: 42 },\\n  { week: 6, signups: 48 },\\n  { week: 7, signups: 46 },\\n  { week: 8, signups: 57 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { useMemo, useState } from 'react'\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport {\n  brushX,\n  type BrushRange,\n  type BrushXChange,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { Chart } from '@tanstack/charts/react'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nconst weeks = rows.map((row) => row.week)\nconst initialRange: BrushRange<number> = { start: 2, end: 6 }\n\nexport default function App() {\n  const [range, setRange] = useState<BrushRange<number>>(initialRange)\n  const definition = useMemo(\n    () =>\n      defineChart({\n        marks: [\n          lineY(rows, {\n            x: 'week',\n            y: 'signups',\n            points: true,\n            stroke: '#2563eb',\n            strokeWidth: 2.5,\n          }),\n        ],\n        scales: {\n          x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([1, 8]) },\n          y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Signups' } },\n        },\n\n        controls: [\n          brushX({\n            range: controlledSignal<BrushRange<number>, BrushXChange<number>>(\n              range,\n              (next, { reason }) => {\n                if (reason.type === 'commit') setRange(next)\n              },\n            ),\n            values: weeks,\n            format: (week) => `Week ${week}`,\n            ariaLabel: 'Reporting range',\n            startAriaLabel: 'Range start',\n            endAriaLabel: 'Range end',\n          }),\n        ],\n      }),\n    [range],\n  )\n\n  const isInitial =\n    range.start === initialRange.start && range.end === initialRange.end\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      <Chart\n        definition={definition}\n        height={280}\n        ariaLabel=\"Weekly signups with a selectable reporting range\"\n      />\n      <p role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n        Current range: weeks {range.start}–{range.end}\n      </p>\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        disabled={isInitial}\n        onClick={() => setRange({ ...initialRange })}\n      >\n        Reset range\n      </button>\n    </section>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx","meta":"group=controlled-brush env=charts-react file=/src/App.tsx entry","title":"/src/App.tsx","file":"/src/App.tsx"},{"type":"code","value":"export const rows = [\n  { week: 1, signups: 18 },\n  { week: 2, signups: 24 },\n  { week: 3, signups: 31 },\n  { week: 4, signups: 29 },\n  { week: 5, signups: 42 },\n  { week: 6, signups: 48 },\n  { week: 7, signups: 46 },\n  { week: 8, signups: 57 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=controlled-brush file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":" defines semantic order, snapping, and keyboard steps. It is required\nfor strings and for keyboard handles. Number and Date brushes may omit it only\nwith "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyboard: false"},{"type":"text","value":" and an invertible scale. The range remains non-null: a\nblank click proposes a snapped zero-width range, which application policy can\nexpand into a fixed focus window."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Change reasons distinguish pointer previews, commits, and cancellation from\nkeyboard commits and cancellation. Rebuild the definition with the accepted\nrange. The behavior retains active controlled echoes, cancels divergent\nexternal updates, normalizes reverse drags, clamps to the final plot, and\nignores synthetic D3 move events."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG and Canvas DOM hosts replace the static scene fallback with one accessible\nbrush overlay. Static SVG and React Native retain the visual selection only;\nnative applications must supply their own semantic range control. Import\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-brush"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-selection"},{"type":"text","value":" directly only for a different application-owned\ngesture."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/89-brush-range-selection/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the monthly time-series brush catalog case"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scale-bound-handle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scale-bound handle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"handleX"},{"type":"text","value":" for one ordered value that the reader can drag or move with a\nkeyboard, such as a playback position:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  handleX,\n  type HandleXChange,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: utcScale },\n    y: null,\n  },\n  controls: [\n    handleX({\n      value: controlledSignal<Date, HandleXChange<Date>>(currentDate, (next) =>\n        setCurrentDate(next),\n      ),\n      values: observedDates,\n      cross: { edge: 'bottom', offset: 8 },\n      ariaLabel: 'Playback position',\n      format: (date) => dayFormat(date),\n    }),\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":" is the ordered snapping and keyboard domain. The controlled value\nmust be one of those candidates. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cross"},{"type":"text","value":" places the track above or below the\nplot, or at a semantic y value with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ value }"},{"type":"text","value":". The default vertical rule\nconnects the track and plot; set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ruleStyle: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to omit it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pointer and touch movement proposes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preview"},{"type":"text","value":" changes. Release proposes a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":"; cancellation proposes the gesture origin. Arrow keys move one\ncandidate and Home or End selects an endpoint. Every keyboard move commits\nimmediately. The SVG and Canvas DOM hosts provide one named horizontal slider\nwith a 44-pixel hit target by default. Static SVG and React Native paint the\naccepted track, rule, and handle but require an application-owned semantic\ncontrol for input."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Charts owns final-scale positioning, nearest-candidate snapping, pointer\ncapture, cancellation, keyboard movement, painting, and host teardown. The\napplication owns playback clocks, play/pause controls, status text, and\npersistence."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"zoom-and-pan","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zoom and pan"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep zoom state as a semantic window, not an opaque DOM transform. Import the\noptional first-party behavior and bind it to the same window used by the x\nscale:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  zoomX,\n  type ZoomXChange,\n  type ZoomXWindow,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: utcScale.copy().domain([window.start, window.end]) },\n    y: null,\n  },\n  controls: [\n    zoomX({\n      window: controlledSignal<ZoomXWindow<Date>, ZoomXChange<Date>>(\n        window,\n        (next) => setWindow(next),\n      ),\n      extent: fullExtent,\n      scaleExtent: [1, 8],\n      ariaLabel: 'Zoomable revenue window',\n      format: (date) => dayFormat(date),\n    }),\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extent"},{"type":"text","value":" is the complete allowed x domain. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleExtent"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[1, maximum]"},{"type":"text","value":" and\ndefaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[1, Infinity]"},{"type":"text","value":". The behavior owns final-scale inversion,\npointer-anchored wheel zoom, drag and horizontal-wheel pan, touch input,\nkeyboard zoom and pan, clamping, cancellation, and teardown. By default, it\ncaptures the wheel only after its plot surface receives focus. Set\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wheelActivation: 'modifier'"},{"type":"text","value":" to capture Control+wheel or Command+wheel without\nprior focus while leaving every unmodified wheel to the page. Set it to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"always"},{"type":"text","value":" only when every wheel over the plot should be chart-owned."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every proposal is a complete number or Date window. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ZoomXChange"},{"type":"text","value":" distinguishes\ngesture "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preview"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel"},{"type":"text","value":" events, includes the gesture origin,\nand records its action and source. Rebuild the definition with every accepted\npreview for live movement; a cancel proposes the origin. Keyboard changes\ncommit immediately."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep visible-row filtering or clipping, y-domain policy, status, reset and\nrecovery controls, follow-latest behavior, and persistence in the application.\nA reset updates the same controlled window. Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-zoom"},{"type":"text","value":" directly only\nwhen the application needs a different gesture policy."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"linked-views","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Linked views"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Store one semantic cursor, selection, or domain and derive every view from it.\nFor focus cursors, share a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" controller with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"match: 'x'"},{"type":"text","value":" or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"match: 'y'"},{"type":"text","value":". Each view can have an independent opposite-axis scale while\nsharing a date or category."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validate outgoing events by semantic value, not matching pixel positions.\nDifferent chart sizes and margins should still resolve the same selection."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"timelines-and-editors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Timelines and editors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scrubbers and editable ranges should pair direct manipulation with native\ncontrols:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"range input for a playhead;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"two range inputs or date inputs for an interval;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Play/Pause and Reset buttons;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"visible duration or current-frame text;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Escape or Cancel for reversible edits;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"live announcements for committed changes."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The chart renders the controlled state. It does not become the form control."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scrollable-lanes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scrollable lanes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For resource timelines, native horizontal overflow is often better than\ncapturing the wheel:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep the lane label rail fixed;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"let the timeline region scroll;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"preserve viewport-relative geometry after updates;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep task details reachable by keyboard;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"avoid clipping axis labels at the scroll boundary."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An interaction controller may install pointer capture, event listeners,\nobservers, nested chart hosts, or animation frames. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRender"},{"type":"text","value":" can update an\nexisting controller, but the application surface must destroy every resource\nwhen the chart unmounts or ownership changes. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" itself owns\nno platform resources; hosts unsubscribe when destroyed. A host clears only\nthe exact unpinned state object that it most recently published. If the\napplication or another host has replaced the controller state, cancellation,\nrebinding, or unmount leaves that newer state intact. Pinned state also\nsurvives lifecycle cleanup until an explicit dismissal or programmatic clear."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"interaction-checklist","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interaction checklist"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State is semantic and controlled."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Crosshair presentation is derived from focus or a cursor binding instead of\nDOM mutation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Geometry comes from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.chart"},{"type":"text","value":" and configured scale copies."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart-owned focus is disabled only when another complete interaction owns the\nsurface."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pointer, keyboard, and touch reach equivalent outcomes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wheel capture does not unexpectedly trap page scrolling."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dragging survives out-of-bounds movement and cancellation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Range limits, snapping, and reset are explicit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State remains valid after data and size updates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External listeners, overlays, and nested hosts are destroyed."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-owner","text":"Choose the owner","level":2},{"id":"cursors-and-crosshairs","text":"Cursors and crosshairs","level":2},{"id":"synchronize-focus-by-value","text":"Synchronize focus by value","level":3},{"id":"track-free-coordinates","text":"Track free coordinates","level":3},{"id":"controlled-signals","text":"Controlled signals","level":2},{"id":"controlled-keyed-selection","text":"Controlled keyed selection","level":2},{"id":"continuous-cursor","text":"Continuous cursor","level":2},{"id":"custom-interaction-loop","text":"Custom interaction loop","level":2},{"id":"controlled-point-inspection","text":"Controlled point inspection","level":2},{"id":"invert-resolved-scales","text":"Invert resolved scales","level":2},{"id":"disable-competing-datum-focus","text":"Disable competing datum focus","level":2},{"id":"brush-selection","text":"Brush selection","level":2},{"id":"scale-bound-handle","text":"Scale-bound handle","level":2},{"id":"zoom-and-pan","text":"Zoom and pan","level":2},{"id":"linked-views","text":"Linked views","level":2},{"id":"timelines-and-editors","text":"Timelines and editors","level":2},{"id":"scrollable-lanes","text":"Scrollable lanes","level":2},{"id":"lifecycle","text":"Lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"interaction-checklist","text":"Interaction checklist","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Charts owns datum focus, selection, crosshair presentation, optional\ncursor bindings, and the mechanics of explicitly imported chart controls. The\napplication owns accepted semantic values, shared controller identity, and\nproduct policy.\n\nThis boundary keeps the default host small and lets applications use\nbattle-tested interaction controllers only when needed.\n\n## Choose the owner\n\nUse native chart focus for:\n\n- nearest-point inspection;\n- grouped axis tooltips;\n- snapped crosshairs;\n- synchronized focus cursors;\n- keyboard point navigation;\n- point activation.\n\nUse a first-party controlled chart control for:\n\n- categorical series visibility through `interactiveColorLegend`;\n- semantic point selection through `keyedSelection`;\n- an unsnapped numeric or temporal plot position through `continuousCursor`;\n- one ordered scale value through `handleX`;\n- a scale-bound horizontal range through `brushX`;\n- a controlled numeric or temporal x window through `zoomX`;\n- other controls that explicitly accept a `ControlledSignal`.\n\nUse controlled application state for:\n\n- shared cursor-controller identity and programmatic cursor values;\n- accepted brush, zoom, and pan domains;\n- focus-and-context windows;\n- synchronized-view layout and domain policy;\n- scrollable resource lanes;\n- playback timing and play/pause controls;\n- editable intervals;\n- rich pinned tooltips.\n\nSee [Tooltips and Focus](./tooltips-and-focus.md) for chart-owned datum inspection.\n\n## Cursors and crosshairs\n\n`crosshair` is presentation; `createChartCursor` is state. A crosshair without\na cursor binding follows the chart's local `ChartFocusState`:\n\n```ts\nimport { crosshair } from '@tanstack/charts/crosshair'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    crosshair({ x: { label: true }, y: false }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  maxFocusDistance: Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY,\n})\n```\n\nThis gives pointer and keyboard users one snapped vertical guide. It does not\ncreate a point, change the focus strategy, or require an SVG overlay.\n\nOn a categorical axis, `x: { band: true }` or `y: { band: true }` replaces\nthat axis rule with a cursor band sized from the resolved scale bandwidth. A\nband options object controls inset, radius, fill, stroke, and opacity. Use\nseparate crosshair marks when the band should paint below the data and the\nother axis rule should paint above it. Zero-bandwidth axes emit no band.\n\n### Synchronize focus by value\n\nShare one controller between definitions when several browser or React Native\ncharts should resolve the same semantic value through their own scales:\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartCursor, cursorHost } from '@tanstack/charts/cursor'\n\nconst sharedDate = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\nconst cursor = {\n  use: cursorHost,\n  controller: sharedDate,\n  mode: 'focus' as const,\n  match: 'x' as const,\n  pin: true,\n}\n\nconst throughputDefinition = defineChart(throughputSpec, { cursor })\nconst errorsDefinition = defineChart(errorsSpec, { cursor })\n```\n\nFocus mode publishes `anchor: 'value'`. Each chart resolves that date to its\nown local point and focus group; no chart copies another chart's pixels.\nPointer, keyboard, restored, and programmatic sources remain visible in the\nshared state. `match` defaults to `xy` and can be `x` or `y`. Focus state may\nalso carry an optional local `origin` point identity to break ties between\nrepeated equal values, including facets. A stable `key` plus `markId` survives\ndata reordering; `datumIndex` disambiguates duplicate keys. A chart ignores the\norigin when its key and mark do not exist locally and resolves from the\nportable semantic `value` and preferred `group`.\n\n### Track free coordinates\n\nFree mode updates without selecting a datum. The host stores normalized plot\ncoordinates and derives semantic values through each resolved scale's inverse:\n\n```ts\nconst freeCursor = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\nconst xScale = scaleUtc().domain([start, end])\nconst yScale = scaleLinear().domain([0, maximum])\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    crosshair({\n      x: { label: true },\n      y: { label: true },\n      marker: true,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  cursor: {\n    use: cursorHost,\n    controller: freeCursor,\n    mode: 'free',\n    pin: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe resolved final ranges own inversion, including a reversed y range. Add an\naxis `valueAt` only to replace that default with observed-value snapping,\nrounding, or another semantic policy. A missing or non-invertible scale\nrequires such an override; returning `undefined` keeps that axis\ncoordinate-only.\n\nSet semantic state directly when another control owns the cursor:\n\n```ts\nfreeCursor.setState({\n  anchor: 'value',\n  value: { x: selectedDate, y: selectedValue },\n  source: 'programmatic',\n  pinned: true,\n})\n\nfreeCursor.setState(null)\n```\n\nWith `pin: true`, click or tap pins the current cursor and another activation\ndismisses it. Browser focus mode also supports Enter, Space, and Escape. React\nChart-owned focus exposes equivalent activate and escape accessibility\nactions. Free mode has no invented keyboard or accessibility point order; pair\nit with labeled date/number inputs, a range control, or textual status when\narbitrary values are part of the reader's task. The crosshair itself is visual\nand `aria-hidden`.\n\nBrowser renderer hosts and React Native `Chart` both bind focus and free\ncursors. They share controller state, semantic focus resolution, coordinate\nprojection, and crosshair presentation; only their event plumbing differs.\nReact Native uses responder gestures and accessibility actions instead of DOM\npointer and key events.\n\nApplication code imports `createChartCursor`, `cursorHost`, and cursor state\ntypes from `@tanstack/charts/cursor`. Every cursor binding supplies\n`use: cursorHost`, keeping cursor policy out of charts that do not opt in. The\nadapter-facing\n`@tanstack/charts/cursor/host` entry contains the platform-neutral projection\nand focus helpers used to implement a host; ordinary chart definitions do not\nneed it.\n\n## Controlled signals\n\nA controlled signal is the boundary between application-owned semantic state\nand a chart-owned behavior:\n\n```ts\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\nconst visible = controlledSignal(visibleSeries, (next, { reason }) => {\n  setVisibleSeries(next)\n})\n```\n\nIt is only a typed snapshot and callback. It does not create a chart store,\nsubscription, or second lifecycle. Rebuild the definition with the accepted\nvalue, as with a controlled form input. The behavior owns interaction details;\nthe application owns persistence and policy.\n\n## Controlled keyed selection\n\nUse `keyedSelection` when chart activation and application UI share one stable\ndatum key:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { keyedSelection, whenSelected } from '@tanstack/charts/selection'\n\nconst selection = keyedSelection<Observation, string, number, number>({\n  selected: controlledSignal(selectedId, (next, { reason }) => {\n    setSelectedId(next)\n  }),\n  key: (datum) => datum.id,\n})\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(observations, {\n      id: 'observations',\n      x: 'flipperLength',\n      y: 'bodyMass',\n      key: 'id',\n    }),\n    whenSelected(\n      dot(observations, {\n        id: 'selected-observation',\n        x: 'flipperLength',\n        y: 'bodyMass',\n        key: 'id',\n        r: 7,\n        strokeWidth: 2,\n      }),\n      selection,\n    ),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  selection,\n})\n```\n\nClick, Enter, and Space propose the selected key through the controlled signal.\nA blank-surface click proposes `null` when a selection exists. The change\nreason distinguishes `select` from `clear` and records whether activation came\nfrom `pointer` or `keyboard`. A nullish key makes that point non-selectable.\n\n`whenSelected` is an ordinary authored mark filtered after scale domains\nresolve. Its complete data and channels still contribute to the domains, but\nonly geometry whose point matches the selected key is painted. The filtered\noverlay is decorative: it emits no second focus or activation point. If one\nlogical point owns several scene fragments, every matching fragment remains.\n\nFocus and selection are independent. Focus can move without changing the\ncontrolled key, and a selected overlay does not replace the normal focus ring.\nKeep semantic tables, status announcements, clear buttons, persistence, and\nproduct-specific key policy in application UI. Those controls can update the\nsame selected value used to rebuild the definition.\n\n## Continuous cursor\n\nUse `continuousCursor` for an arbitrary x/y plot position that must not snap to\na datum:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  continuousCursor,\n  type ContinuousCursorChange,\n  type ContinuousCursorPosition,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\ntype Position = ContinuousCursorPosition<number, number>\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [dot(rows, { x: 'horsepower', y: 'economy' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: horsepowerScale },\n    y: { scale: economyScale },\n  },\n\n  controls: [\n    continuousCursor({\n      position: controlledSignal<\n        Position | null,\n        ContinuousCursorChange<number, number>\n      >(cursorPosition, (next, { reason }) => {\n        if (reason.type === 'commit' || reason.type === 'clear') {\n          setCursorPosition(next)\n        }\n      }),\n      xLabel: { format: (value) => `HP ${value.toFixed(1)}` },\n      yLabel: { format: (value) => `MPG ${value.toFixed(1)}` },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\nBoth scales must be invertible numeric or temporal scales. The behavior uses\ntheir final resolved ranges, including a reversed y range, and clamps values to\nthe plot. This is the same default inversion used by a free `cursorHost`\nbinding. Rules and the marker are enabled by default. Axis labels are opt-in.\n\nA `null` controlled position leaves pointer previews transient. Click or tap\nproposes a `commit`; accepting its non-null position pins the cursor. A second\nactivation or Escape proposes `clear`. Pointer leave and cancellation clear an\nunpinned preview. `ContinuousCursorChange` distinguishes `preview`, `commit`,\nand `clear`, records pointer, touch, or keyboard source, and preserves the\norigin position.\n\nThe SVG and Canvas DOM hosts replace the static scene fallback with a host\noverlay, so pointer movement repaints the guide without rebuilding the chart\nscene. Static SVG and React Native paint an accepted non-null position but do\nnot provide pointer input. Pair the cursor with semantic sliders or inputs and\nvisible status text for keyboard and nonvisual operation. Those controls can\nwrite the same application-owned position.\n\n## Custom interaction loop\n\nUse this lower-level loop when no first-party behavior owns the gesture:\n\n1. Render the definition from semantic state.\n2. Read `scene.chart` and resolved scales in `onRender`.\n3. Convert pointer geometry into semantic values.\n4. Clamp, snap, or validate those values as product policy.\n5. Update application state.\n6. Let the next definition produce the scene.\n\nDo not mutate SVG geometry directly and then attempt to reconcile application\nstate afterward.\n\n## Controlled point inspection\n\nUse the chart's interaction controller when the application owns pointer\ntiming but still wants the definition's focus strategy, focus marks, and\ntooltip. Long-press inspection is one example:\n\n```tsx\nlet interaction: ChartInteractionController<Row, Date, number> | undefined\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', key: 'id' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc() },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear() },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'nearest-x',\n  pointer: false,\n  tooltip,\n})\n\nconst chart = (\n  <Chart\n    definition={definition}\n    ariaLabel=\"Portfolio history\"\n    onRender={(context) => {\n      interaction = context.interaction\n    }}\n  />\n)\n\nfunction inspect(clientX: number, clientY: number) {\n  interaction?.setControlledFocus(interaction.resolvePointer(clientX, clientY))\n}\n\nfunction stopInspecting() {\n  interaction?.setControlledFocus(null)\n}\n```\n\n`resolvePointer` uses the current renderer presentation, including an active\nmotion or viewport transform, and returns the scene position, primary point,\nand complete focus group. `setControlledFocus` paints the same definition-owned\nfocus and tooltip as chart-owned pointer input. Pass `{ pinned: true }` when the\nconfigured sticky tooltip should accept interaction.\n\nFor a drag that does not require a nearby datum, use\n`interaction.clientToScene(clientX, clientY)`. It applies the renderer's full\nclient-to-scene transform without coupling viewport movement to point focus.\n\n`pointer: false` disables automatic pointer move, leave, and click handling. It\ndoes not disable keyboard navigation. Controlled focus has separate ownership,\nso unrelated mouse-leave and focus-out events cannot clear it. The stable\ncontroller is available as `host.interaction` and in every `onRender` context.\n\n## Invert resolved scales\n\nFirst-party behaviors use the final resolved scale. A custom gesture can read\nthe same optional inverse from the scene:\n\n```ts\nconst invertX = scene.scales.x.invert\nif (!invertX) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible x scale')\n\nconst date = invertX(pointerX)\n```\n\nThe resolved y scale already owns its reversed pixel range:\n\n```ts\nconst invertY = scene.scales.y.invert\nif (!invertY) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible y scale')\n\nconst value = invertY(pointerY)\n```\n\nApply UTC month snapping, numeric rounding, minimum ranges, and domain clamps\nafter inversion. Those policies are application semantics, not scale math.\nThis date example uses a D3 time scale; the lightweight linear scale supports\nthe same resolved mapping and inversion flow for numeric gestures.\nFree cursor bindings already consume the resolved inverse. Their `valueAt`\ncallback is reserved for snapping or another semantic override.\n\nThe [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) page is the sole source for\nD3 ownership and official interaction-module links.\n\n## Disable competing datum focus\n\nWhen a gesture has no datum inspection at all, disable chart-owned focus explicitly:\n\n```ts\nimport { focusDisabled } from '@tanstack/charts/focus/disabled'\n\nconst gestureDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  focus: focusDisabled,\n  keyboard: false,\n})\n\nmountChart(element, {\n  definition: gestureDefinition,\n  ariaLabel: 'Selectable monthly range',\n  onRender: mountBrushOverlay,\n})\n```\n\nThis prevents an application-owned gesture from competing with the host's\npoint marker and tooltip. First-party host controls such as `brushX` and\n`zoomX` isolate their own events.\n\nUse `pointer: false` plus the interaction controller when the application owns\nthe gesture but the chart should still own datum focus. `focusDisabled` does\nnot remove keyboard accessibility from application-owned controls.\nA definition `cursor` in `free` mode already owns the host pointer path and\ndoes not require `pointer: false` or `focusDisabled`.\n\n## Brush selection\n\nA complete brush owns:\n\n- drag start, move, end, and cancellation;\n- reverse dragging normalization;\n- semantic snapping;\n- a visible selected range;\n- focusable handles or equivalent range inputs;\n- current-range text;\n- reset behavior;\n- selection preservation after data updates.\n\nImport the optional first-party behavior and bind it to application state:\n\n```tsx group=controlled-brush env=charts-react file=/src/App.tsx entry\nimport { useMemo, useState } from 'react'\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport {\n  brushX,\n  type BrushRange,\n  type BrushXChange,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { Chart } from '@tanstack/charts/react'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nconst weeks = rows.map((row) => row.week)\nconst initialRange: BrushRange<number> = { start: 2, end: 6 }\n\nexport default function App() {\n  const [range, setRange] = useState<BrushRange<number>>(initialRange)\n  const definition = useMemo(\n    () =>\n      defineChart({\n        marks: [\n          lineY(rows, {\n            x: 'week',\n            y: 'signups',\n            points: true,\n            stroke: '#2563eb',\n            strokeWidth: 2.5,\n          }),\n        ],\n        scales: {\n          x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([1, 8]) },\n          y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Signups' } },\n        },\n\n        controls: [\n          brushX({\n            range: controlledSignal<BrushRange<number>, BrushXChange<number>>(\n              range,\n              (next, { reason }) => {\n                if (reason.type === 'commit') setRange(next)\n              },\n            ),\n            values: weeks,\n            format: (week) => `Week ${week}`,\n            ariaLabel: 'Reporting range',\n            startAriaLabel: 'Range start',\n            endAriaLabel: 'Range end',\n          }),\n        ],\n      }),\n    [range],\n  )\n\n  const isInitial =\n    range.start === initialRange.start && range.end === initialRange.end\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      <Chart\n        definition={definition}\n        height={280}\n        ariaLabel=\"Weekly signups with a selectable reporting range\"\n      />\n      <p role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n        Current range: weeks {range.start}–{range.end}\n      </p>\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        disabled={isInitial}\n        onClick={() => setRange({ ...initialRange })}\n      >\n        Reset range\n      </button>\n    </section>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```ts group=controlled-brush file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { week: 1, signups: 18 },\n  { week: 2, signups: 24 },\n  { week: 3, signups: 31 },\n  { week: 4, signups: 29 },\n  { week: 5, signups: 42 },\n  { week: 6, signups: 48 },\n  { week: 7, signups: 46 },\n  { week: 8, signups: 57 },\n]\n```\n\n`values` defines semantic order, snapping, and keyboard steps. It is required\nfor strings and for keyboard handles. Number and Date brushes may omit it only\nwith `keyboard: false` and an invertible scale. The range remains non-null: a\nblank click proposes a snapped zero-width range, which application policy can\nexpand into a fixed focus window.\n\nChange reasons distinguish pointer previews, commits, and cancellation from\nkeyboard commits and cancellation. Rebuild the definition with the accepted\nrange. The behavior retains active controlled echoes, cancels divergent\nexternal updates, normalizes reverse drags, clamps to the final plot, and\nignores synthetic D3 move events.\n\nSVG and Canvas DOM hosts replace the static scene fallback with one accessible\nbrush overlay. Static SVG and React Native retain the visual selection only;\nnative applications must supply their own semantic range control. Import\n`d3-brush` and `d3-selection` directly only for a different application-owned\ngesture.\n\n[Open the monthly time-series brush catalog case](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/89-brush-range-selection/).\n\n## Scale-bound handle\n\nUse `handleX` for one ordered value that the reader can drag or move with a\nkeyboard, such as a playback position:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  handleX,\n  type HandleXChange,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: utcScale },\n    y: null,\n  },\n  controls: [\n    handleX({\n      value: controlledSignal<Date, HandleXChange<Date>>(currentDate, (next) =>\n        setCurrentDate(next),\n      ),\n      values: observedDates,\n      cross: { edge: 'bottom', offset: 8 },\n      ariaLabel: 'Playback position',\n      format: (date) => dayFormat(date),\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n`values` is the ordered snapping and keyboard domain. The controlled value\nmust be one of those candidates. `cross` places the track above or below the\nplot, or at a semantic y value with `{ value }`. The default vertical rule\nconnects the track and plot; set `ruleStyle: false` to omit it.\n\nPointer and touch movement proposes `preview` changes. Release proposes a\n`commit`; cancellation proposes the gesture origin. Arrow keys move one\ncandidate and Home or End selects an endpoint. Every keyboard move commits\nimmediately. The SVG and Canvas DOM hosts provide one named horizontal slider\nwith a 44-pixel hit target by default. Static SVG and React Native paint the\naccepted track, rule, and handle but require an application-owned semantic\ncontrol for input.\n\nCharts owns final-scale positioning, nearest-candidate snapping, pointer\ncapture, cancellation, keyboard movement, painting, and host teardown. The\napplication owns playback clocks, play/pause controls, status text, and\npersistence.\n\n## Zoom and pan\n\nKeep zoom state as a semantic window, not an opaque DOM transform. Import the\noptional first-party behavior and bind it to the same window used by the x\nscale:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  zoomX,\n  type ZoomXChange,\n  type ZoomXWindow,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: utcScale.copy().domain([window.start, window.end]) },\n    y: null,\n  },\n  controls: [\n    zoomX({\n      window: controlledSignal<ZoomXWindow<Date>, ZoomXChange<Date>>(\n        window,\n        (next) => setWindow(next),\n      ),\n      extent: fullExtent,\n      scaleExtent: [1, 8],\n      ariaLabel: 'Zoomable revenue window',\n      format: (date) => dayFormat(date),\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n`extent` is the complete allowed x domain. `scaleExtent` is `[1, maximum]` and\ndefaults to `[1, Infinity]`. The behavior owns final-scale inversion,\npointer-anchored wheel zoom, drag and horizontal-wheel pan, touch input,\nkeyboard zoom and pan, clamping, cancellation, and teardown. By default, it\ncaptures the wheel only after its plot surface receives focus. Set\n`wheelActivation: 'modifier'` to capture Control+wheel or Command+wheel without\nprior focus while leaving every unmodified wheel to the page. Set it to\n`always` only when every wheel over the plot should be chart-owned.\n\nEvery proposal is a complete number or Date window. `ZoomXChange` distinguishes\ngesture `preview`, `commit`, and `cancel` events, includes the gesture origin,\nand records its action and source. Rebuild the definition with every accepted\npreview for live movement; a cancel proposes the origin. Keyboard changes\ncommit immediately.\n\nKeep visible-row filtering or clipping, y-domain policy, status, reset and\nrecovery controls, follow-latest behavior, and persistence in the application.\nA reset updates the same controlled window. Import `d3-zoom` directly only\nwhen the application needs a different gesture policy.\n\n## Linked views\n\nStore one semantic cursor, selection, or domain and derive every view from it.\nFor focus cursors, share a `createChartCursor` controller with `match: 'x'` or\n`match: 'y'`. Each view can have an independent opposite-axis scale while\nsharing a date or category.\n\nValidate outgoing events by semantic value, not matching pixel positions.\nDifferent chart sizes and margins should still resolve the same selection.\n\n## Timelines and editors\n\nScrubbers and editable ranges should pair direct manipulation with native\ncontrols:\n\n- range input for a playhead;\n- two range inputs or date inputs for an interval;\n- Play/Pause and Reset buttons;\n- visible duration or current-frame text;\n- Escape or Cancel for reversible edits;\n- live announcements for committed changes.\n\nThe chart renders the controlled state. It does not become the form control.\n\n## Scrollable lanes\n\nFor resource timelines, native horizontal overflow is often better than\ncapturing the wheel:\n\n- keep the lane label rail fixed;\n- let the timeline region scroll;\n- preserve viewport-relative geometry after updates;\n- keep task details reachable by keyboard;\n- avoid clipping axis labels at the scroll boundary.\n\n## Lifecycle\n\nAn interaction controller may install pointer capture, event listeners,\nobservers, nested chart hosts, or animation frames. `onRender` can update an\nexisting controller, but the application surface must destroy every resource\nwhen the chart unmounts or ownership changes. `createChartCursor` itself owns\nno platform resources; hosts unsubscribe when destroyed. A host clears only\nthe exact unpinned state object that it most recently published. If the\napplication or another host has replaced the controller state, cancellation,\nrebinding, or unmount leaves that newer state intact. Pinned state also\nsurvives lifecycle cleanup until an explicit dismissal or programmatic clear.\n\n## Interaction checklist\n\n- State is semantic and controlled.\n- Crosshair presentation is derived from focus or a cursor binding instead of\n  DOM mutation.\n- Geometry comes from `scene.chart` and configured scale copies.\n- Chart-owned focus is disabled only when another complete interaction owns the\n  surface.\n- Pointer, keyboard, and touch reach equivalent outcomes.\n- Wheel capture does not unexpectedly trap page scrolling.\n- Dragging survives out-of-bounds movement and cancellation.\n- Range limits, snapping, and reset are explicit.\n- State remains valid after data and size updates.\n- External listeners, overlays, and nested hosts are destroyed.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Interactions and Selections\ndescription: Build controlled cursors, linked selections, brushes, zooming, scrolling, timelines, and editors around semantic chart state.\n---\n\nTanStack Charts owns datum focus, selection, crosshair presentation, optional\ncursor bindings, and the mechanics of explicitly imported chart controls. The\napplication owns accepted semantic values, shared controller identity, and\nproduct policy.\n\nThis boundary keeps the default host small and lets applications use\nbattle-tested interaction controllers only when needed.\n\n## Choose the owner\n\nUse native chart focus for:\n\n- nearest-point inspection;\n- grouped axis tooltips;\n- snapped crosshairs;\n- synchronized focus cursors;\n- keyboard point navigation;\n- point activation.\n\nUse a first-party controlled chart control for:\n\n- categorical series visibility through `interactiveColorLegend`;\n- semantic point selection through `keyedSelection`;\n- an unsnapped numeric or temporal plot position through `continuousCursor`;\n- one ordered scale value through `handleX`;\n- a scale-bound horizontal range through `brushX`;\n- a controlled numeric or temporal x window through `zoomX`;\n- other controls that explicitly accept a `ControlledSignal`.\n\nUse controlled application state for:\n\n- shared cursor-controller identity and programmatic cursor values;\n- accepted brush, zoom, and pan domains;\n- focus-and-context windows;\n- synchronized-view layout and domain policy;\n- scrollable resource lanes;\n- playback timing and play/pause controls;\n- editable intervals;\n- rich pinned tooltips.\n\nSee [Tooltips and Focus](./tooltips-and-focus.md) for chart-owned datum inspection.\n\n## Cursors and crosshairs\n\n`crosshair` is presentation; `createChartCursor` is state. A crosshair without\na cursor binding follows the chart's local `ChartFocusState`:\n\n```ts\nimport { crosshair } from '@tanstack/charts/crosshair'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    crosshair({ x: { label: true }, y: false }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'group-x',\n  maxFocusDistance: Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY,\n})\n```\n\nThis gives pointer and keyboard users one snapped vertical guide. It does not\ncreate a point, change the focus strategy, or require an SVG overlay.\n\nOn a categorical axis, `x: { band: true }` or `y: { band: true }` replaces\nthat axis rule with a cursor band sized from the resolved scale bandwidth. A\nband options object controls inset, radius, fill, stroke, and opacity. Use\nseparate crosshair marks when the band should paint below the data and the\nother axis rule should paint above it. Zero-bandwidth axes emit no band.\n\n### Synchronize focus by value\n\nShare one controller between definitions when several browser or React Native\ncharts should resolve the same semantic value through their own scales:\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartCursor, cursorHost } from '@tanstack/charts/cursor'\n\nconst sharedDate = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\nconst cursor = {\n  use: cursorHost,\n  controller: sharedDate,\n  mode: 'focus' as const,\n  match: 'x' as const,\n  pin: true,\n}\n\nconst throughputDefinition = defineChart(throughputSpec, { cursor })\nconst errorsDefinition = defineChart(errorsSpec, { cursor })\n```\n\nFocus mode publishes `anchor: 'value'`. Each chart resolves that date to its\nown local point and focus group; no chart copies another chart's pixels.\nPointer, keyboard, restored, and programmatic sources remain visible in the\nshared state. `match` defaults to `xy` and can be `x` or `y`. Focus state may\nalso carry an optional local `origin` point identity to break ties between\nrepeated equal values, including facets. A stable `key` plus `markId` survives\ndata reordering; `datumIndex` disambiguates duplicate keys. A chart ignores the\norigin when its key and mark do not exist locally and resolves from the\nportable semantic `value` and preferred `group`.\n\n### Track free coordinates\n\nFree mode updates without selecting a datum. The host stores normalized plot\ncoordinates and derives semantic values through each resolved scale's inverse:\n\n```ts\nconst freeCursor = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\nconst xScale = scaleUtc().domain([start, end])\nconst yScale = scaleLinear().domain([0, maximum])\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    crosshair({\n      x: { label: true },\n      y: { label: true },\n      marker: true,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  cursor: {\n    use: cursorHost,\n    controller: freeCursor,\n    mode: 'free',\n    pin: true,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe resolved final ranges own inversion, including a reversed y range. Add an\naxis `valueAt` only to replace that default with observed-value snapping,\nrounding, or another semantic policy. A missing or non-invertible scale\nrequires such an override; returning `undefined` keeps that axis\ncoordinate-only.\n\nSet semantic state directly when another control owns the cursor:\n\n```ts\nfreeCursor.setState({\n  anchor: 'value',\n  value: { x: selectedDate, y: selectedValue },\n  source: 'programmatic',\n  pinned: true,\n})\n\nfreeCursor.setState(null)\n```\n\nWith `pin: true`, click or tap pins the current cursor and another activation\ndismisses it. Browser focus mode also supports Enter, Space, and Escape. React\nChart-owned focus exposes equivalent activate and escape accessibility\nactions. Free mode has no invented keyboard or accessibility point order; pair\nit with labeled date/number inputs, a range control, or textual status when\narbitrary values are part of the reader's task. The crosshair itself is visual\nand `aria-hidden`.\n\nBrowser renderer hosts and React Native `Chart` both bind focus and free\ncursors. They share controller state, semantic focus resolution, coordinate\nprojection, and crosshair presentation; only their event plumbing differs.\nReact Native uses responder gestures and accessibility actions instead of DOM\npointer and key events.\n\nApplication code imports `createChartCursor`, `cursorHost`, and cursor state\ntypes from `@tanstack/charts/cursor`. Every cursor binding supplies\n`use: cursorHost`, keeping cursor policy out of charts that do not opt in. The\nadapter-facing\n`@tanstack/charts/cursor/host` entry contains the platform-neutral projection\nand focus helpers used to implement a host; ordinary chart definitions do not\nneed it.\n\n## Controlled signals\n\nA controlled signal is the boundary between application-owned semantic state\nand a chart-owned behavior:\n\n```ts\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\nconst visible = controlledSignal(visibleSeries, (next, { reason }) => {\n  setVisibleSeries(next)\n})\n```\n\nIt is only a typed snapshot and callback. It does not create a chart store,\nsubscription, or second lifecycle. Rebuild the definition with the accepted\nvalue, as with a controlled form input. The behavior owns interaction details;\nthe application owns persistence and policy.\n\n## Controlled keyed selection\n\nUse `keyedSelection` when chart activation and application UI share one stable\ndatum key:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { keyedSelection, whenSelected } from '@tanstack/charts/selection'\n\nconst selection = keyedSelection<Observation, string, number, number>({\n  selected: controlledSignal(selectedId, (next, { reason }) => {\n    setSelectedId(next)\n  }),\n  key: (datum) => datum.id,\n})\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(observations, {\n      id: 'observations',\n      x: 'flipperLength',\n      y: 'bodyMass',\n      key: 'id',\n    }),\n    whenSelected(\n      dot(observations, {\n        id: 'selected-observation',\n        x: 'flipperLength',\n        y: 'bodyMass',\n        key: 'id',\n        r: 7,\n        strokeWidth: 2,\n      }),\n      selection,\n    ),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  selection,\n})\n```\n\nClick, Enter, and Space propose the selected key through the controlled signal.\nA blank-surface click proposes `null` when a selection exists. The change\nreason distinguishes `select` from `clear` and records whether activation came\nfrom `pointer` or `keyboard`. A nullish key makes that point non-selectable.\n\n`whenSelected` is an ordinary authored mark filtered after scale domains\nresolve. Its complete data and channels still contribute to the domains, but\nonly geometry whose point matches the selected key is painted. The filtered\noverlay is decorative: it emits no second focus or activation point. If one\nlogical point owns several scene fragments, every matching fragment remains.\n\nFocus and selection are independent. Focus can move without changing the\ncontrolled key, and a selected overlay does not replace the normal focus ring.\nKeep semantic tables, status announcements, clear buttons, persistence, and\nproduct-specific key policy in application UI. Those controls can update the\nsame selected value used to rebuild the definition.\n\n## Continuous cursor\n\nUse `continuousCursor` for an arbitrary x/y plot position that must not snap to\na datum:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  continuousCursor,\n  type ContinuousCursorChange,\n  type ContinuousCursorPosition,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/cursor'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\ntype Position = ContinuousCursorPosition<number, number>\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [dot(rows, { x: 'horsepower', y: 'economy' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: horsepowerScale },\n    y: { scale: economyScale },\n  },\n\n  controls: [\n    continuousCursor({\n      position: controlledSignal<\n        Position | null,\n        ContinuousCursorChange<number, number>\n      >(cursorPosition, (next, { reason }) => {\n        if (reason.type === 'commit' || reason.type === 'clear') {\n          setCursorPosition(next)\n        }\n      }),\n      xLabel: { format: (value) => `HP ${value.toFixed(1)}` },\n      yLabel: { format: (value) => `MPG ${value.toFixed(1)}` },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\nBoth scales must be invertible numeric or temporal scales. The behavior uses\ntheir final resolved ranges, including a reversed y range, and clamps values to\nthe plot. This is the same default inversion used by a free `cursorHost`\nbinding. Rules and the marker are enabled by default. Axis labels are opt-in.\n\nA `null` controlled position leaves pointer previews transient. Click or tap\nproposes a `commit`; accepting its non-null position pins the cursor. A second\nactivation or Escape proposes `clear`. Pointer leave and cancellation clear an\nunpinned preview. `ContinuousCursorChange` distinguishes `preview`, `commit`,\nand `clear`, records pointer, touch, or keyboard source, and preserves the\norigin position.\n\nThe SVG and Canvas DOM hosts replace the static scene fallback with a host\noverlay, so pointer movement repaints the guide without rebuilding the chart\nscene. Static SVG and React Native paint an accepted non-null position but do\nnot provide pointer input. Pair the cursor with semantic sliders or inputs and\nvisible status text for keyboard and nonvisual operation. Those controls can\nwrite the same application-owned position.\n\n## Custom interaction loop\n\nUse this lower-level loop when no first-party behavior owns the gesture:\n\n1. Render the definition from semantic state.\n2. Read `scene.chart` and resolved scales in `onRender`.\n3. Convert pointer geometry into semantic values.\n4. Clamp, snap, or validate those values as product policy.\n5. Update application state.\n6. Let the next definition produce the scene.\n\nDo not mutate SVG geometry directly and then attempt to reconcile application\nstate afterward.\n\n## Controlled point inspection\n\nUse the chart's interaction controller when the application owns pointer\ntiming but still wants the definition's focus strategy, focus marks, and\ntooltip. Long-press inspection is one example:\n\n```tsx\nlet interaction: ChartInteractionController<Row, Date, number> | undefined\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value', key: 'id' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc() },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear() },\n  },\n\n  focus: 'nearest-x',\n  pointer: false,\n  tooltip,\n})\n\nconst chart = (\n  <Chart\n    definition={definition}\n    ariaLabel=\"Portfolio history\"\n    onRender={(context) => {\n      interaction = context.interaction\n    }}\n  />\n)\n\nfunction inspect(clientX: number, clientY: number) {\n  interaction?.setControlledFocus(interaction.resolvePointer(clientX, clientY))\n}\n\nfunction stopInspecting() {\n  interaction?.setControlledFocus(null)\n}\n```\n\n`resolvePointer` uses the current renderer presentation, including an active\nmotion or viewport transform, and returns the scene position, primary point,\nand complete focus group. `setControlledFocus` paints the same definition-owned\nfocus and tooltip as chart-owned pointer input. Pass `{ pinned: true }` when the\nconfigured sticky tooltip should accept interaction.\n\nFor a drag that does not require a nearby datum, use\n`interaction.clientToScene(clientX, clientY)`. It applies the renderer's full\nclient-to-scene transform without coupling viewport movement to point focus.\n\n`pointer: false` disables automatic pointer move, leave, and click handling. It\ndoes not disable keyboard navigation. Controlled focus has separate ownership,\nso unrelated mouse-leave and focus-out events cannot clear it. The stable\ncontroller is available as `host.interaction` and in every `onRender` context.\n\n## Invert resolved scales\n\nFirst-party behaviors use the final resolved scale. A custom gesture can read\nthe same optional inverse from the scene:\n\n```ts\nconst invertX = scene.scales.x.invert\nif (!invertX) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible x scale')\n\nconst date = invertX(pointerX)\n```\n\nThe resolved y scale already owns its reversed pixel range:\n\n```ts\nconst invertY = scene.scales.y.invert\nif (!invertY) throw new Error('This interaction requires an invertible y scale')\n\nconst value = invertY(pointerY)\n```\n\nApply UTC month snapping, numeric rounding, minimum ranges, and domain clamps\nafter inversion. Those policies are application semantics, not scale math.\nThis date example uses a D3 time scale; the lightweight linear scale supports\nthe same resolved mapping and inversion flow for numeric gestures.\nFree cursor bindings already consume the resolved inverse. Their `valueAt`\ncallback is reserved for snapping or another semantic override.\n\nThe [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) page is the sole source for\nD3 ownership and official interaction-module links.\n\n## Disable competing datum focus\n\nWhen a gesture has no datum inspection at all, disable chart-owned focus explicitly:\n\n```ts\nimport { focusDisabled } from '@tanstack/charts/focus/disabled'\n\nconst gestureDefinition = defineChart(definition, {\n  focus: focusDisabled,\n  keyboard: false,\n})\n\nmountChart(element, {\n  definition: gestureDefinition,\n  ariaLabel: 'Selectable monthly range',\n  onRender: mountBrushOverlay,\n})\n```\n\nThis prevents an application-owned gesture from competing with the host's\npoint marker and tooltip. First-party host controls such as `brushX` and\n`zoomX` isolate their own events.\n\nUse `pointer: false` plus the interaction controller when the application owns\nthe gesture but the chart should still own datum focus. `focusDisabled` does\nnot remove keyboard accessibility from application-owned controls.\nA definition `cursor` in `free` mode already owns the host pointer path and\ndoes not require `pointer: false` or `focusDisabled`.\n\n## Brush selection\n\nA complete brush owns:\n\n- drag start, move, end, and cancellation;\n- reverse dragging normalization;\n- semantic snapping;\n- a visible selected range;\n- focusable handles or equivalent range inputs;\n- current-range text;\n- reset behavior;\n- selection preservation after data updates.\n\nImport the optional first-party behavior and bind it to application state:\n\n```tsx group=controlled-brush env=charts-react file=/src/App.tsx entry\nimport { useMemo, useState } from 'react'\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport {\n  brushX,\n  type BrushRange,\n  type BrushXChange,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/brush'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { Chart } from '@tanstack/charts/react'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nconst weeks = rows.map((row) => row.week)\nconst initialRange: BrushRange<number> = { start: 2, end: 6 }\n\nexport default function App() {\n  const [range, setRange] = useState<BrushRange<number>>(initialRange)\n  const definition = useMemo(\n    () =>\n      defineChart({\n        marks: [\n          lineY(rows, {\n            x: 'week',\n            y: 'signups',\n            points: true,\n            stroke: '#2563eb',\n            strokeWidth: 2.5,\n          }),\n        ],\n        scales: {\n          x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([1, 8]) },\n          y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Signups' } },\n        },\n\n        controls: [\n          brushX({\n            range: controlledSignal<BrushRange<number>, BrushXChange<number>>(\n              range,\n              (next, { reason }) => {\n                if (reason.type === 'commit') setRange(next)\n              },\n            ),\n            values: weeks,\n            format: (week) => `Week ${week}`,\n            ariaLabel: 'Reporting range',\n            startAriaLabel: 'Range start',\n            endAriaLabel: 'Range end',\n          }),\n        ],\n      }),\n    [range],\n  )\n\n  const isInitial =\n    range.start === initialRange.start && range.end === initialRange.end\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      <Chart\n        definition={definition}\n        height={280}\n        ariaLabel=\"Weekly signups with a selectable reporting range\"\n      />\n      <p role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n        Current range: weeks {range.start}–{range.end}\n      </p>\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        disabled={isInitial}\n        onClick={() => setRange({ ...initialRange })}\n      >\n        Reset range\n      </button>\n    </section>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```ts group=controlled-brush file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { week: 1, signups: 18 },\n  { week: 2, signups: 24 },\n  { week: 3, signups: 31 },\n  { week: 4, signups: 29 },\n  { week: 5, signups: 42 },\n  { week: 6, signups: 48 },\n  { week: 7, signups: 46 },\n  { week: 8, signups: 57 },\n]\n```\n\n`values` defines semantic order, snapping, and keyboard steps. It is required\nfor strings and for keyboard handles. Number and Date brushes may omit it only\nwith `keyboard: false` and an invertible scale. The range remains non-null: a\nblank click proposes a snapped zero-width range, which application policy can\nexpand into a fixed focus window.\n\nChange reasons distinguish pointer previews, commits, and cancellation from\nkeyboard commits and cancellation. Rebuild the definition with the accepted\nrange. The behavior retains active controlled echoes, cancels divergent\nexternal updates, normalizes reverse drags, clamps to the final plot, and\nignores synthetic D3 move events.\n\nSVG and Canvas DOM hosts replace the static scene fallback with one accessible\nbrush overlay. Static SVG and React Native retain the visual selection only;\nnative applications must supply their own semantic range control. Import\n`d3-brush` and `d3-selection` directly only for a different application-owned\ngesture.\n\n[Open the monthly time-series brush catalog case](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/89-brush-range-selection/).\n\n## Scale-bound handle\n\nUse `handleX` for one ordered value that the reader can drag or move with a\nkeyboard, such as a playback position:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  handleX,\n  type HandleXChange,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/handle'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: utcScale },\n    y: null,\n  },\n  controls: [\n    handleX({\n      value: controlledSignal<Date, HandleXChange<Date>>(currentDate, (next) =>\n        setCurrentDate(next),\n      ),\n      values: observedDates,\n      cross: { edge: 'bottom', offset: 8 },\n      ariaLabel: 'Playback position',\n      format: (date) => dayFormat(date),\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n`values` is the ordered snapping and keyboard domain. The controlled value\nmust be one of those candidates. `cross` places the track above or below the\nplot, or at a semantic y value with `{ value }`. The default vertical rule\nconnects the track and plot; set `ruleStyle: false` to omit it.\n\nPointer and touch movement proposes `preview` changes. Release proposes a\n`commit`; cancellation proposes the gesture origin. Arrow keys move one\ncandidate and Home or End selects an endpoint. Every keyboard move commits\nimmediately. The SVG and Canvas DOM hosts provide one named horizontal slider\nwith a 44-pixel hit target by default. Static SVG and React Native paint the\naccepted track, rule, and handle but require an application-owned semantic\ncontrol for input.\n\nCharts owns final-scale positioning, nearest-candidate snapping, pointer\ncapture, cancellation, keyboard movement, painting, and host teardown. The\napplication owns playback clocks, play/pause controls, status text, and\npersistence.\n\n## Zoom and pan\n\nKeep zoom state as a semantic window, not an opaque DOM transform. Import the\noptional first-party behavior and bind it to the same window used by the x\nscale:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  zoomX,\n  type ZoomXChange,\n  type ZoomXWindow,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom'\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: utcScale.copy().domain([window.start, window.end]) },\n    y: null,\n  },\n  controls: [\n    zoomX({\n      window: controlledSignal<ZoomXWindow<Date>, ZoomXChange<Date>>(\n        window,\n        (next) => setWindow(next),\n      ),\n      extent: fullExtent,\n      scaleExtent: [1, 8],\n      ariaLabel: 'Zoomable revenue window',\n      format: (date) => dayFormat(date),\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n`extent` is the complete allowed x domain. `scaleExtent` is `[1, maximum]` and\ndefaults to `[1, Infinity]`. The behavior owns final-scale inversion,\npointer-anchored wheel zoom, drag and horizontal-wheel pan, touch input,\nkeyboard zoom and pan, clamping, cancellation, and teardown. By default, it\ncaptures the wheel only after its plot surface receives focus. Set\n`wheelActivation: 'modifier'` to capture Control+wheel or Command+wheel without\nprior focus while leaving every unmodified wheel to the page. Set it to\n`always` only when every wheel over the plot should be chart-owned.\n\nEvery proposal is a complete number or Date window. `ZoomXChange` distinguishes\ngesture `preview`, `commit`, and `cancel` events, includes the gesture origin,\nand records its action and source. Rebuild the definition with every accepted\npreview for live movement; a cancel proposes the origin. Keyboard changes\ncommit immediately.\n\nKeep visible-row filtering or clipping, y-domain policy, status, reset and\nrecovery controls, follow-latest behavior, and persistence in the application.\nA reset updates the same controlled window. Import `d3-zoom` directly only\nwhen the application needs a different gesture policy.\n\n## Linked views\n\nStore one semantic cursor, selection, or domain and derive every view from it.\nFor focus cursors, share a `createChartCursor` controller with `match: 'x'` or\n`match: 'y'`. Each view can have an independent opposite-axis scale while\nsharing a date or category.\n\nValidate outgoing events by semantic value, not matching pixel positions.\nDifferent chart sizes and margins should still resolve the same selection.\n\n## Timelines and editors\n\nScrubbers and editable ranges should pair direct manipulation with native\ncontrols:\n\n- range input for a playhead;\n- two range inputs or date inputs for an interval;\n- Play/Pause and Reset buttons;\n- visible duration or current-frame text;\n- Escape or Cancel for reversible edits;\n- live announcements for committed changes.\n\nThe chart renders the controlled state. It does not become the form control.\n\n## Scrollable lanes\n\nFor resource timelines, native horizontal overflow is often better than\ncapturing the wheel:\n\n- keep the lane label rail fixed;\n- let the timeline region scroll;\n- preserve viewport-relative geometry after updates;\n- keep task details reachable by keyboard;\n- avoid clipping axis labels at the scroll boundary.\n\n## Lifecycle\n\nAn interaction controller may install pointer capture, event listeners,\nobservers, nested chart hosts, or animation frames. `onRender` can update an\nexisting controller, but the application surface must destroy every resource\nwhen the chart unmounts or ownership changes. `createChartCursor` itself owns\nno platform resources; hosts unsubscribe when destroyed. A host clears only\nthe exact unpinned state object that it most recently published. If the\napplication or another host has replaced the controller state, cancellation,\nrebinding, or unmount leaves that newer state intact. Pinned state also\nsurvives lifecycle cleanup until an explicit dismissal or programmatic clear.\n\n## Interaction checklist\n\n- State is semantic and controlled.\n- Crosshair presentation is derived from focus or a cursor binding instead of\n  DOM mutation.\n- Geometry comes from `scene.chart` and configured scale copies.\n- Chart-owned focus is disabled only when another complete interaction owns the\n  surface.\n- Pointer, keyboard, and touch reach equivalent outcomes.\n- Wheel capture does not unexpectedly trap page scrolling.\n- Dragging survives out-of-bounds movement and cancellation.\n- Range limits, snapping, and reset are explicit.\n- State remains valid after data and size updates.\n- External listeners, overlays, and nested hosts are destroyed.\n"}
